.Dd August 17, 2026
.Dt SOWA-CHROOT 8
.Os Sowa
.Sh NAME
.Nm sowa-chroot
.Nd enter an installed Sowa system with its kernel filesystems in place
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Ar destination
.Op Ar command Op Ar argument ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Xr chroot 8
alone changes the root directory and nothing else, which leaves the new root
without
.Pa /proc ,
.Pa /sys
or
.Pa /dev .
Almost everything worth running in there needs at least one of them:
.Xr grub-install 8
reads
.Pa /sys
and
.Pa /dev
to find the disk it is being pointed at,
.Xr sowa-pkg 8
wants
.Pa /dev/urandom ,
and
.Xr ps 1
and
.Xr mount 8
read
.Pa /proc .
.Pp
.Nm
mounts the four of them, runs a shell - or the command given after the
destination - and unmounts them again on the way out, in reverse order and
whether the shell exited cleanly or not.
A filesystem something inside is still holding is detached lazily rather than
left bind-mounted under a directory forever.
.Pp
With no
.Ar command
it runs
.Ic /bin/bash -l ,
so the shell reads
.Pa /etc/profile
and the
.Ev PATH
and prompt inside the chroot are its own.
With a command, it runs that one and leaves.
The exit status is the command's.
.Pp
What is mounted, and why each is what it is:
.Bl -tag -width /dev -offset indent
.It Pa /proc , Pa /sys
mounted fresh rather than bound.
They are the same filesystem wherever they appear and a second mount costs
nothing.
.It Pa /dev
bound recursively from the host, and made a slave mount.
A fresh devtmpfs would carry the device nodes but not
.Pa /dev/pts ,
which is a separate filesystem mounted over it, and without a pty there is no
job control in the chroot's shell.
The slave marking is what keeps the recursive unmount from propagating back out
and taking the host's
.Pa /dev/pts
with it.
.It Pa /run
a tmpfs of its own, not a bind.
.Pa /run
holds the state of the processes running
.Em now ,
and the chroot's are not those.
This is also what the installed system gets from
.Pa rc.sysinit
at boot, so anything run in here sees the
.Pa /run
it would see for real.
.It Pa /sys/firmware/efi/efivars
only when the host itself booted UEFI, and only because
.Xr grub-install 8
writes an NVRAM boot entry through it.
.El
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width "-h, --help"
.It Fl h , Fl -help
Print usage and exit.
.El
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width SOWA_INSTALL_LIB
.It Ev SOWA_INSTALL_LIB
Where to find
.Pa install-functions .
The default is
.Pa /usr/lib/sowa .
.It Ev SOWA_INSTALL_IGNORE_KERNEL
Set to 1 to run on a kernel older than 6.1 anyway; see
.Xr sowa-bootstrap 8 .
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
The exit status is the command's, or the shell's.
.Nm
itself exits 1 when it will not enter the destination at all.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Finish an install from inside it:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
sowa-chroot /mnt
.Ed
.Pp
One command, and out again:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
sowa-chroot /mnt grub-install --target=i386-pc /dev/sda
sowa-chroot /mnt grub-mkconfig -o /boot/grub/grub.cfg
sowa-chroot /mnt passwd
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr sowa-bootstrap 8 ,
.Xr sowa-pkg 8 ,
.Xr sowa-setup 8
.Sh CAVEATS
Must run as root.
.Pp
The destination has to hold a Sowa system - what
.Xr sowa-setup 8
or
.Xr sowa-bootstrap 8
leaves behind - because the alternative is bind-mounting
.Pa /dev
over a directory that was a typo.
It does not have to be a mount point; a tree bootstrapped into a plain
directory is entered with a warning and nothing more.
.Pp
This is a chroot, not a container.
Everything inside shares the host's kernel, its process table and its network,
and runs as root over the host's own
.Pa /dev .
